# What must never enter a build context.
#
# Two jobs. The first is speed: `node_modules` and `dist` are hundreds of
# megabytes that the build regenerates anyway, and every byte here is uploaded
# to the daemon before the first instruction runs.
#
# The second is the one that matters. **A build context is not a layer, but
# anything COPYed from it becomes one** — and a layer is extractable by anyone
# who can pull the image. `secrets/` holds JWT keys and Firebase service
# accounts; `.env` holds real credentials. Both are gitignored, which protects
# the repository and does nothing at all for an image build.

node_modules
**/node_modules
dist
**/dist
coverage
**/coverage
.turbo
**/.turbo

# **Both patterns.** A bare `*.tsbuildinfo` matches only the repo root, so the
# nested ones (`libs/common/tsconfig.build.tsbuildinfo`) were copied into the
# build context while `dist/` was excluded — and `tsc --incremental` then reads
# a buildinfo saying "everything is up to date", emits NOTHING, and the next
# package fails with "has no exported member" for every symbol in the library
# that was just built successfully.
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Credentials. See above — gitignore does not apply to a build context.
secrets/
**/secrets/
.env
**/.env
**/.env.*
# The Firebase service account, which is the one credential that does not
# reliably sit under `secrets/`. auth-service's contract points into it
# (`FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PATH = ./secrets/serviceAccountKey.json`) but
# storage-service's does not (`./serviceAccountKey.json`, at the package ROOT),
# and a copy exists at BOTH package roots on disk. The pattern is unanchored so
# it holds wherever the file lands, which is the point — the `secrets/` rule
# above only covers one of the two locations.
# It was gitignored and NOT listed here, so it entered the context and landed in
# the `prune` stage's `COPY . .` layer. It never reached a shipped image —
# `turbo prune` emits only git-tracked files, so it is absent from `out/full`,
# and `runtime` copies only `package.json` and `dist` — but `--target build` or
# a registry cache export would have published it. The rule this file opens with
# is that credentials do not enter a build context; this is the line that makes
# it true of all of them.
**/serviceAccountKey.json

# Python local state, so a developer's virtualenv never lands in an image.
.venv
**/.venv
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache

.git
.github
docs
.vscode
.claude
# Deployment manifests. Nothing in any stage COPYs them, and the context is
# uploaded to the daemon before the first instruction runs.
k8s
